   "format": "short_answer",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Consider the curve x^2 - 6x + 4y^2 + 8y = 3, for which dy/dx = (3 - x)/(4y + 4). The curve has two points at which the tangent line is vertical. Find the greater of their two x-coordinates."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 7
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A vertical tangent needs the denominator of dy/dx to be zero with the numerator nonzero: 4y + 4 = 0 gives y = -1.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            1
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Substitute y = -1 into the curve: x^2 - 6x + 4 - 8 = 3, so x^2 - 6x - 7 = 0 and (x - 7)(x + 1) = 0.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            [
               "Subtract",
               "x",
               7
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Both x = 7 and x = -1 give a nonzero numerator, 3 - 7 = -4 and 3 + 1 = 4, so both points (7, -1) and (-1, -1) have vertical tangents.",
         "mathjson": -4
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The greater x-coordinate is 7.",
         "mathjson": 7
      }
   ],
